Type: propriety audit, since the concern is that ₹2,000 crore R&D spend is wasteful and unjustified on the test of public interest although rules were followed. Propriety audit verifies transactions on the tests of public interest, commonly accepted customs and standards of conduct.

Principles:
(i) expenditure not prima facie more than the occasion demands; officials exercise the vigilance of a person of ordinary prudence over his own money;
(ii) sanctioning authority does not pass an order accruing to its own advantage;
(iii) funds not used for the benefit of a particular person or group;
(iv) no indirect benefit to management or employees beyond agreed remuneration.

Functions of the auditor in propriety audit:
• see that all expenditure is properly planned;
• see that the size and channels of expenditure are rightful and expected to give maximum results;
• appraise whether the expenditure is likely to give optimum results;
• see whether a substitute plan could improve current operations and return on capital expenditure;
• examine management's actions and decisions to see they are conducive to public interest and meet standards of conduct.

Likely objectives:
• whether proper planning and processes captured beneficiary data;
• whether steps were taken to prevent payment delays, pilferage and duplication;
• whether DBT infrastructure, organisation and management were adequate and effective.

Audit criteria: standards used to determine whether a programme meets or exceeds expectations; reasonable and attainable standards against which economy, efficiency and effectiveness are assessed.

Here: Government of India and state circulars, instructions, SOP manuals, scheme guidelines on identification and authentication of beneficiaries, and general management and subject literature.

Committee on Public Undertakings (COPU). Functions:
(i) examine reports and accounts of public undertakings;
(ii) examine C&AG reports on public undertakings;
(iii) examine autonomy and efficiency and whether they are managed on sound business principles and prudent commercial practices;
(iv) other PAC / Estimates Committee functions not covered above, allotted by the Speaker.
Its examination is a comprehensive appraisal of the undertaking's policies, programmes and financial working.
